essentials","k":"atm","la":-36.845375,"lo":174.773234,"d":614,"h":"Mo-Su 07:00-22:00"},{"n":"Lotus","g":"essentials","k":"bureau_de_change","la":-36.848729,"lo":174.765611,"d":648},{"n":"First World War Memorial Beacon","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-36.842881,"lo":174.76593,"d":96,"ds":"The First World War Memorial Beacon is a war memorial on Quay Street in Auckland, the largest city in New Zealand. New Zealand's first built World War I memorial, it was first unveiled in 1915, before being lost in the 1960s. It was rediscovered and restored in 1999–2000, at which time it was placed outside the New Zealand Maritime Museum.","wpu":"First World War Memorial Beacon","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/NZ_AK_First_World_War_Memorial_Beacon.jpg/960px-NZ_AK_First_World_War_Memorial_Beacon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Commercial Bay Shopping Centre","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-36.84378,"lo":174.76628,"d":107,"ds":"Commercial Bay Shopping Centre is a shopping centre in the Auckland CBD, Auckland, New Zealand. It is situated at 11–19 Customs Street West between Lower Albert Street and Te Komititanga square, and is opposite Waitematā railway station. The centre replaced a precinct that was known as Downtown Shopping Centre, formerly Westfield Downtown.","wpu":"Commercial Bay Shopping Centre","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/CommercialBayAucklandH%26M.jpg/960px-CommercialBayAucklandH%26M.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Auckland waterfront","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-36.8427,"lo":174.764292,"d":243,"ds":"The Auckland waterfront (rarely the Auckland harbourfront) is a city-side stretch of the southern Waitematā Harbour coastline in Auckland, New Zealand. Previously mostly dominated by Ports of Auckland uses, from the 2000s on it is becoming increasingly open to recreational public use, with a number of former wharves being converted to office, entertainment, and later also some residential uses.","wpu":"Auckland waterfront","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/1-NZ-Auckland%2C-HSBC.jpg/960px-1-NZ-Auckland%2C-HSBC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"New Zealand Maritime Museum","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-36.8417,"lo":174.7634,"d":351,"ds":"The New Zealand Maritime Museum Hui Te Ananui a Tangaroa is a maritime museum in Auckland, New Zealand. It is located on Hobson Wharf, adjacent to the Viaduct Harbour in central Auckland. It houses exhibitions spanning New Zealand's maritime history, from the first Polynesian explorers and settlers to modern day triumphs at the America's Cup.","wpu":"New Zealand Maritime Museum","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/New_Zealand_Maritime_Museum.jpg/960px-New_Zealand_Maritime_Museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Gus Fisher Gallery","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-36.84669,"lo":174.769363,"d":461,"ds":"Gus Fisher Gallery is a contemporary art gallery owned and operated by the University of Auckland in Auckland, New Zealand. It is located in the Kenneth Myers Centre, a historic building restored in 2000 with the help of the gallery's patron, Gus Fisher (1920–2010).","wpu":"Gus Fisher Gallery","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Kenneth_Myers_Centre.jpg/960px-Kenneth_Myers_Centre.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Point Britomart","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-36.844049,"lo":174.772178,"d":475,"ds":"Point Britomart (Māori: Te Rerenga Ora Iti) was a headland in the Waitematā Harbour, in Auckland, New Zealand. Located between Commercial Bay and Official Bay, the point was later quarried away to produce fill for land reclamation in Mechanics Bay, and almost no physical trace remains at street level in what is today an area of the Auckland CBD and the Auckland waterfront.","wpu":"Point Britomart","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/da/Official_Bay_And_Point_Britomart.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Ellen Melville Centre","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-36.8476,"lo":174.7668,"d":511,"ds":"The Ellen Melville Centre is a community centre located in Freyberg Square in central Auckland, New Zealand. It is named after Ellen Melville, the first woman to be elected to the Auckland City Council and the second New Zealand woman to become a lawyer.","wpu":"Ellen Melville Centre","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Walking_Tour_Edit-a-thon_May_2024_8.jpg/960px-Walking_Tour_Edit-a-thon_May_2024_8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Landmark House","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-36.84824,"lo":174.76524,"d":603,"ds":"Landmark House is a neo-Gothic building located at 187–189 Queen Street in Auckland, New Zealand. It was originally designed for the Auckland Electrical Power Board (AEPB) and constructed between 1928 and 1930. The architects responsible for the design were Alva Bartley and Thomas Edward Norman (Norman) Wade.","wpu":"Landmark House","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Building_-_Auckland%2C_NZ_-_DSC07020.jpg/960px-Building_-_Auckland%2C_NZ_-_DSC07020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Albert Park Volcano","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-36.848585,"lo":174.767263,"d":621,"ds":"The Albert Park Volcano was one of the volcanoes in the Auckland volcanic field in New Zealand. A small volcano that erupted approximately 145,000 years ago, the volcanic remnants were quarried during the early colonial history of Auckland between 1840 and 1869.","wpu":"Albert Park Volcano","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/10/AucklandMapHochstetter1859_-_Albert_Park_and_Grafton_Volcanoes_crop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Viaduct Harbour","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-36.843534,"lo":174.760015,"d":624,"ds":"Viaduct Harbour, formerly known as Viaduct Basin, is a former commercial harbour on the Auckland waterfront that has been turned into a development of mostly upscale apartments, office space and restaurants. It is located on the site of a formerly run-down area of the Freemans Bay / Auckland CBD waterfront in Auckland, New Zealand.","wpu":"Viaduct Harbour","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Viaduct_Basin_Auckland_01.jpg/960px-Viaduct_Basin_Auckland_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Wynyard Crossing","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-36.841284,"lo":174.759824,"d":666,"ds":"Wynyard Crossing (also known as Te Wero Bridge) is a modern double bascule pedestrian and cyclists' bridge built in 2011 in Auckland, New Zealand. It connects the redeveloped Wynyard Quarter with Te Wero Island and the existing Viaduct Harbour entertainment district, and thereby, with the wider Auckland waterfront.","wpu":"Wynyard Crossing","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Wynyard_Crossing_%26_Auckland_Skyline.jpg/960px-Wynyard_Crossing_%26_Auckland_Skyline.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Horotiu (sculpture)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-36.849012,"lo":174.765154,"d":688,"ds":"Horotiu is a bronze sculpture located on the corner of Queen Street and Victoria Street West in Auckland, New Zealand created by Chris Bailey and Sally Smith. It depicts a 7.2 metre long spiraling tuna kūwharuwhara (New Zealand longfin eel). It was unveiled on 29 July 2026.","wpu":"Horotiu (sculpture)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Horotiu_%28sculpture%29.jpg/960px-Horotiu_%28sculpture%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sky Tower (Auckland)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-36.84847,"lo":174.76231,"d":737,"ds":"The Sky Tower is a telecommunications and observation tower in Auckland, New Zealand.","wpu":"Sky Tower (Auckland)","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f8/01_Auckland_New_Zealand-1000137.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Statue of Queen Victoria, Auckland","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-36.85025,"lo":174.768083,"d":811,"ds":"A statue of Queen Victoria was erected at Albert Park, Auckland, in 1899. The second monument to Queen Victoria in New Zealand, the bronze statue was funded by public donations encouraged by Auckland politicians and the New Zealand Herald in celebration of the Queen's Diamond Jubilee.","wpu":"Statue of Queen Victoria, Auckland","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Queen_Victoria_in_Albert_Park.jpg/960px-Queen_Victoria_in_Albert_Park.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Khartoum Place","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-36.850698,"lo":174.765851,"d":862,"ds":"Khartoum Place is a pedestrianised city square in the Auckland CBD, New Zealand. The square, protected by several mature trees, is located between Lorne Street and Kitchener Street, and provides a stairway connection between the two street levels. The Auckland Art Gallery is located at the Kitchener Street end of the square, with other related exhibition and public space also arrayed around the square.","wpu":"Khartoum Place","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Khartoum_Place3.JPG/960px-Khartoum_Place3.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Auckland is a large metropolitan city in the North Island of New Zealand. It has an urban population of approximately 1,547,200 (June 2025). It is located in the greater Auckland Region, the area governed by Auckland Council, which includes outlying rural areas and the islands of the Hauraki Gulf, and which has a total population of 1,816,000 as of June 2025.","wpu":"Auckland","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Auckland_skyline_-_May_2024_%282%29.jpg/960px-Auckland_skyline_-_May_2024_%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}